<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <PackageId>Com.H.Net.Ssh</PackageId>
    <AssemblyName>Com.H.Net.Ssh</AssemblyName>
    <Authors>Hussein Al Bayati</Authors>
    <Description>Wrapper to SSH.NET API by Renci.
Introduces minor new functionality (upload / download folders and subfolders, etc..)
Includes full async/await support on every target, including .NET Standard 2.0 (.NET Framework 4.6.2+).</Description>
    <Copyright>Hussein Al Bayati</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/H7O/Com.H.Net.Ssh</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageIcon>128x128.jpg</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>nuget_readme.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/H7O/Com.H.Net.Ssh</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>SFTP;Async;SSH</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>10.2.1

* Fixed: two uploads into the same new remote folder at once could make one of them fail. Upload
  and UploadAsync create the folders above the file when they are missing, and a folder another
  client created between the check and the create made the create throw. They now accept a folder
  that exists by then.

10.2.0 added SFtpClient.Rename and RenameAsync, and raised the dependency floors to 10.0.13:
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H.Net.Ssh/10.2.0#releasenotes-body-tab

Upgrading from 10.0.x or 2.x? 10.1.0 merged the netstandard2.0 and net8.0+ packages into one and
raised the dependency floors (including SSH.NET 2026.0.0). Its notes list every change:
https://www.nuget.org/packages/Com.H.Net.Ssh/10.1.0#releasenotes-body-tab</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <AssemblyVersion>10.2.1.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>10.2.1.0</FileVersion>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>True</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
    <RootNamespace>Com.H.Net.Ssh</RootNamespace>
    <Version>10.2.1</Version>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!-- Deterministic builds for reproducibility -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true' or '$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

    <!-- Source Link - enables stepping into source code when debugging -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>

    <!-- Embed debug symbols in the assembly (avoids a separate .snupkg) -->
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

    <!-- Put the source itself in the PDB, not just a Source Link URL pointing at it.
         Source Link alone is enough only when the compiler rewrites source paths to the "/_/"
         form that the Source Link map covers, and that rewrite is a case-sensitive prefix match
         against the repository root. Parallel inner builds of a multi-targeted project can hand
         MSBuild the project directory with a differently-cased drive letter ("c:\" vs "C:\"), and
         when they do, the rewrite silently does not apply for that one target framework: the map
         still says "/_/*" while the documents say "C:\code\...". Stepping then fails for that
         target only, with nothing in the build output to indicate it. Embedding the sources makes
         stepping independent of that, and of GitHub being reachable at all. This library is four
         files, so the cost is a few KB. -->
    <EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Open-ended range: 2026.0.0 is the floor (the first release carrying the fix for
         GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284), with no ceiling, so a consumer is free to resolve forward to any
         newer SSH.NET without waiting on a release of this package.
         Two things worth knowing when revisiting this:
         - NuGet resolves to the LOWEST version satisfying a range, so a consumer that asks for
           nothing else gets exactly 2026.0.0. Raising this floor is what moves consumers forward.
         - "[2026.0.0,)" and a bare "2026.0.0" mean the same thing to NuGet, and pack normalises
           this to the bare form. Seeing "2026.0.0" in the published .nuspec is expected and does
           not mean the version was pinned. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SSH.NET" Version="[2026.0.0,)" />

    <!-- Not used by this library's own code. It is referenced only to raise the floor on a
         dependency SSH.NET brings in transitively: SSH.NET asks for 8.0.3, which sits on the
         .NET 8 servicing line, and .NET 8 leaves support in November 2026. Once a line is out
         of support a newly discovered vulnerability gets no patched release on it, so consumers
         would have nowhere to upgrade to. Pointing at the .NET 10 line keeps that escape hatch
         open. Raising this also pulls Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions
         (8.0.2 -> 10.0.13) along with it, so that one needs no reference of its own.
         Drop this if SSH.NET ever stops depending on it. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="[10.0.13,)" />

    <!-- Also not used directly. SSH.NET brings this in on every target at 10.0.10; referenced
         here only to hold the floor at the latest release. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Hashing" Version="[10.0.13,)" />

    <!-- Source Link for GitHub. PrivateAssets="All" keeps it out of the .nuspec entirely, so it
         never reaches a consumer and its version is a build-time concern only. Left pinned rather
         than ranged so builds stay reproducible. Since it never ships, it need not match the
         version the other Com.H packages use. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" Version="10.0.402" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Polyfill package for netstandard2.0. Brings IAsyncDisposable, IAsyncEnumerable/IAsyncEnumerator,
       ValueTask and EnumeratorCancellationAttribute, which the async API surface in SFtpClient needs
       and which netstandard2.0 does not carry.
       Other packages a consumer references (Com.H, Com.H.Data.Common) may ask for a different
       minimum, which is fine and needs no coordination. When several packages request the same
       dependency as a minimum, NuGet resolves to the HIGHEST of those minimums, so every request is
       satisfied. Only an exact pin, such as "[10.0.10]", could conflict here. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces" Version="[10.0.13,)" />

    <!-- Neither is used directly. Both are pulled in by SSH.NET at 10.0.10 on the legacy path and
         are referenced here only to hold the floor at the latest release. They live in this group
         because a .NET Framework consumer reads this package's netstandard2.0 dependency group:
         System.Formats.Asn1 reaches it via SSH.NET's netstandard2.0 group and
         Microsoft.Bcl.Cryptography via SSH.NET's net462 group. Neither is in the net8.0+ graphs,
         so keeping them out of the unconditional group spares modern consumers the references. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Formats.Asn1" Version="[10.0.13,)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.Cryptography" Version="[10.0.13,)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="128x128.jpg" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
    <None Include="nuget_readme.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
